### Matrix Multiplication Function in C

To multiply two matrices, the number of columns in the first matrix must match the number of rows in the second matrix. Let the first matrix be $A$ of dimensions $m \times n$, and the second matrix $B$ of dimensions $n \times p$. The resulting matrix $C$ will have dimensions $m \times p$.

Below is the dynamic function implementation in C:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

// Function to allocate and multiply two matrices dynamically
int** multiplyMatrices(int** A, int** B, int m, int n, int p) {
    // Allocate memory for the resultant m x p matrix
    int** C = (int**)malloc(m * sizeof(int*));
    for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
        C[i] = (int*)malloc(p * sizeof(int));
    }

    // Perform matrix multiplication
    for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < p; j++) {
            C[i][j] = 0; // Initialize cell to 0
            for (int k = 0; k < n; k++) {
                C[i][j] += A[i][k] * B[k][j];
            }
        }
    }
    return C;
}
```
